WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.778
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4420.

2
00:00:00.778 --> 00:00:01.556
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:01.556 --> 00:00:02.333
<v ELIZABETH GELVIN>We’re sounding okay?

4
00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:02.852
<v CHARLOTTE WILLCOX>It’s good.

5
00:00:02.852 --> 00:00:04.667
Just try not to hit the wire.

6
00:00:04.667 --> 00:00:04.926
<v GELVIN>Yeah.

7
00:00:04.926 --> 00:00:05.185
Okay.

8
00:00:05.185 --> 00:00:05.444
Cool.

9
00:00:05.444 --> 00:00:05.704
<v WILLCOX>Alright.

10
00:00:05.704 --> 00:00:07.000
We’re ready to go.

11
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:07.846
<v GELVIN>You ready?

12
00:00:07.846 --> 00:00:08.269
<v RUFUS VICTORIAN>Okay.

13
00:00:08.269 --> 00:00:08.692
<v GELVIN>Okay.

14
00:00:08.692 --> 00:00:09.115
Great.

15
00:00:09.115 --> 00:00:12.077
Alright well today is January twenty-third, 2015.

16
00:00:12.077 --> 00:00:13.769
My name’s Elizabeth Gelvin.

17
00:00:13.769 --> 00:00:20.538
I’m representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

18
00:00:20.538 --> 00:00:28.577
We’re working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum and Bill Shearman to document the history of Mossville [Louisiana].

19
00:00:28.577 --> 00:00:37.462
And to kick that off, we have oral history day today here at the Rigmaiden Recreational Center on Old Spanish Trail.

20
00:00:37.462 --> 00:00:40.000
Okay and your name is . . .?

21
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:40.681
<v VICTORIAN>Rufus Victorian.

22
00:00:40.681 --> 00:00:41.363
<v GELVIN>Rufus Victorian.

23
00:00:41.363 --> 00:00:43.747
So I’m here today with Rufus Victorian.

24
00:00:43.747 --> 00:00:45.110
Thanks for being interviewed.

25
00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:49.198
This is going to be a short interview, about ten, fifteen minutes,

26
00:00:49.198 --> 00:00:58.055
and we’ll likely be in touch with you later to set up a longer interview if you would like to continue after the short interview, okay?

27
00:00:58.055 --> 00:01:03.165
So to start off would you mind saying your full name again for the audience?

28
00:01:03.165 --> 00:01:04.187
<v VICTORIAN>Rufus Clarence Victorian.

29
00:01:04.187 --> 00:01:05.209
<v GELVIN>Rufus Clarence Victorian.

30
00:01:05.209 --> 00:01:05.549
Okay.

31
00:01:05.549 --> 00:01:07.934
And when and where were you born?

32
00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:08.615
<v VICTORIAN>Eunice, Louisiana.

33
00:01:08.615 --> 00:01:09.297
<v GELVIN>In Eunice.

34
00:01:09.297 --> 00:01:10.319
<v VICTORIAN>St. Landry Parish.

35
00:01:10.319 --> 00:01:11.000
Yeah.

36
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.600
<v GELVIN>St. Landry . . . What is your date of birth?

37
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:15.950
<v VICTORIAN>January twenty-sixth, 1949.

38
00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:16.400
<v GELVIN>1949.

39
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.100
<v VICTORIAN>Went to first grade St. Matilda . . .

40
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:20.000
<v GELVIN>In Eunice?

41
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:20.900
<v VICTORIAN>Catholic School.

42
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:21.350
Yeah.

43
00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:21.800
<v GELVIN>Okay.

44
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:33.950
<v VICTORIAN>Then went to Charles Drew for about . . . once you get out of the first group you go to Charles Drew which is down the street in Eunice.

45
00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:37.100
Where the nuns taught at St. Matilda.

46
00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:38.000
Okay . . .

47
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.887
<v GELVIN>What brought you to Mossville and when did you come to Mossville?

48
00:01:41.887 --> 00:01:48.366
<v VICTORIAN>Oh, I have aunts . . . The Ledets, the [Carreys?] and my family live here so my parents decided to moved here.

49
00:01:48.366 --> 00:01:50.634
I was like seventeen, eighteen years old.

50
00:01:50.634 --> 00:01:51.282
<v GELVIN>Seventeen eighteen.

51
00:01:51.282 --> 00:01:54.521
<v VICTORIAN>And I’d never seen a crab or nothing like that.

52
00:01:54.521 --> 00:01:56.465
<v GELVIN>You’d never seen a crab before?

53
00:01:56.465 --> 00:01:58.408
<v VICTORIAN>We was like in the country.

54
00:01:58.408 --> 00:01:59.056
<v GELVIN>Got you.

55
00:01:59.056 --> 00:02:01.000
Not a lot of seafood.

56
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:05.379
<v VICTORIAN>Where we lived in Eunice I was . . . I remember Hurricane Audrey coming through.

57
00:02:05.379 --> 00:02:11.105
I was at that age where it picked up our house off the piers and moved it.

58
00:02:11.105 --> 00:02:11.442
<v GELVIN>Wow.

59
00:02:11.442 --> 00:02:14.474
<v VICTORIAN>And we lived right by . . . It was a cinema.

60
00:02:14.474 --> 00:02:17.505
They watch . . . You could see it from the house.

61
00:02:17.505 --> 00:02:19.189
You could watch the movies.

62
00:02:19.189 --> 00:02:21.211
Right on the outskirt of Eunice.

63
00:02:21.211 --> 00:02:26.600
We lived in the country and you could see the cinema, but it knocked that down.

64
00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:27.947
And I remember that.

65
00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:30.642
My daddy getting on his knees and praying.

66
00:02:30.642 --> 00:02:33.000
<v GELVIN>In the middle of the storm?

67
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.111
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah during the storm.

68
00:02:34.111 --> 00:02:35.778
So I remember that very well.

69
00:02:35.778 --> 00:02:37.722
<v GELVIN>How old were you for Hurricane Audrey?

70
00:02:37.722 --> 00:02:40.222
<v VICTORIAN>I was like about . . . eight maybe at the time.

71
00:02:40.222 --> 00:02:40.500
<v GELVIN>Eight?

72
00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:40.778
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah.

73
00:02:40.778 --> 00:02:43.000
<v GELVIN>What were your parents’ and grandparents’ names?

74
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:44.779
<v VICTORIAN>My dad was Clarence Victorian.

75
00:02:44.779 --> 00:02:50.827
That’s why I got the . . . My mom was [Dolena?] Victorian, and my aunt was Annie Mae Ledet.

76
00:02:50.827 --> 00:02:54.385
<v GELVIN>And she lived here in Mossville before you moved up?

77
00:02:54.385 --> 00:02:56.163
<v VICTORIAN>No, they lived in Eunice.

78
00:02:56.163 --> 00:02:56.875
<v GELVIN>Oh, oh.

79
00:02:56.875 --> 00:02:57.587
In Eunice.

80
00:02:57.587 --> 00:02:57.942
Okay.

81
00:02:57.942 --> 00:02:58.298
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah.

82
00:02:58.298 --> 00:03:02.923
Okay, but my mother then moved here to be with her other sister.

83
00:03:02.923 --> 00:03:05.058
<v GELVIN>Well, what’s your mother’s parents’ names?

84
00:03:05.058 --> 00:03:06.481
What were your parents’ . . .?

85
00:03:06.481 --> 00:03:07.904
<v VICTORIAN>She was a Ledet.

86
00:03:07.904 --> 00:03:08.615
<v GELVIN>A Ledet.

87
00:03:08.615 --> 00:03:08.971
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah.

88
00:03:08.971 --> 00:03:11.462
Annie Mae Victorian Ledet . . . Well, Ledet yeah.

89
00:03:11.462 --> 00:03:12.173
Annie Mae.

90
00:03:12.173 --> 00:03:12.529
<v GELVIN>Okay.

91
00:03:12.529 --> 00:03:17.510
So you said you moved to Mossville when you were about seventeen or eighteen?

92
00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:17.865
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah.

93
00:03:17.865 --> 00:03:18.933
<v GELVIN>With your parents?

94
00:03:18.933 --> 00:03:19.288
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah.

95
00:03:19.288 --> 00:03:20.000
Right.

96
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.310
<v GELVIN>Did you finish up high school here?

97
00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:24.950
<v VICTORIAN>No. I didn’t finish high school at all.

98
00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:27.920
I just went to work because we couldn’t afford . . .

99
00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:29.900
<v GELVIN>Did you work here in Mossville?

100
00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:37.160
<v VICTORIAN>No, I went to work in Texas with my brother and he got me on a pipeline and I learned a lot.

101
00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:39.470
Then I went to . . . Got a trade.

102
00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:40.460
<v GELVIN>What’s your trade?

103
00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:41.120
<v VICTORIAN>A pipefitter.

104
00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:41.450
<v GELVIN>Pipefitter.

105
00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:43.430
<v VICTORIAN>I done that for thirty-one years.

106
00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:43.760
<v GELVIN>Wow.

107
00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:44.090
<v VICTORIAN>[Agrees].

108
00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:47.060
So yeah I went to work with my brother.

109
00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:49.370
He lied . . . We lied about my age.

110
00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:52.340
Them days you didn’t need to have all that.

111
00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:53.000
<v GELVIN>Yeah.

112
00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.723
<v VICTORIAN>Went to work on some pipeline . . . Came back.

113
00:03:55.723 --> 00:03:58.785
And I work at Olin [Mandel?] for thirty-one years.

114
00:03:58.785 --> 00:03:59.806
I work construction.

115
00:03:59.806 --> 00:04:00.827
<v GELVIN>In Lake Charles?

116
00:04:00.827 --> 00:04:01.848
<v VICTORIAN>No, right here.

117
00:04:01.848 --> 00:04:03.550
<v GELVIN>Oh, right here in Mossville.

118
00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:03.890
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah.

119
00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:04.911
Well, Mossville, yeah.

120
00:04:04.911 --> 00:04:05.592
Lake Charles.

121
00:04:05.592 --> 00:04:05.932
Yeah.

122
00:04:05.932 --> 00:04:07.293
But that’s the story.

123
00:04:07.293 --> 00:04:12.738
Like I said when I first come here we so country I never saw a crab.

124
00:04:12.738 --> 00:04:13.759
I was impressed.

125
00:04:13.759 --> 00:04:16.482
We went fishing, crabbing . . . We had the beach.

126
00:04:16.482 --> 00:04:19.204
We thought we was like in California somewhere.

127
00:04:19.204 --> 00:04:20.906
<v GELVIN>May as well be, right?

128
00:04:20.906 --> 00:04:23.288
<v VICTORIAN>But Mossville, it’s been good to me.

129
00:04:23.288 --> 00:04:29.754
It’s . . . when I first come here, they had a guy . . . Well, we’d see this down home all the time.

130
00:04:29.754 --> 00:04:37.921
They had a guy, Pete Moss, he’d ride his mule and his horse right down the middle of the road with all his dogs.

131
00:04:37.921 --> 00:04:39.283
Him and his wife.

132
00:04:39.283 --> 00:04:40.304
It was funny.

133
00:04:40.304 --> 00:04:48.471
And they had a little . . . I had cousins . . . we had a little canteen right here down the road over here right off the road.

134
00:04:48.471 --> 00:04:51.534
Barbershop was right by the railroad tracks over here.

135
00:04:51.534 --> 00:04:55.277
And you’d see people . . . they had . . . All the trees was moss.

136
00:04:55.277 --> 00:04:58.000
I thought that’s why they called it . . .

137
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.000
<v GELVIN>With the Spanish moss hanging from it?

138
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:00.286
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah.

139
00:05:00.286 --> 00:05:00.857
Well, everywhere.

140
00:05:00.857 --> 00:05:02.571
You don’t see that no more.

141
00:05:02.571 --> 00:05:04.000
I don’t know what happened . . .

142
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:04.286
<v GELVIN>Yeah.

143
00:05:04.286 --> 00:05:06.286
<v VICTORIAN>But you don’t see that no more.

144
00:05:06.286 --> 00:05:09.714
You’ll see people standing on the main road over here selling mayhaw.

145
00:05:09.714 --> 00:05:12.000
Mayhaw season they have a lot of mayhaws.

146
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:13.143
They’d sell them all.

147
00:05:13.143 --> 00:05:13.429
<v GELVIN>Yeah.

148
00:05:13.429 --> 00:05:14.286
Mayhaws are delicious.

149
00:05:14.286 --> 00:05:14.571
Definitely.

150
00:05:14.571 --> 00:05:14.857
<v VICTORIAN>Right.

151
00:05:14.857 --> 00:05:18.000
And they’d can them and do different things with them.

152
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:18.211
<v GELVIN>Yeah.

153
00:05:18.211 --> 00:05:19.474
How many siblings did you have?

154
00:05:19.474 --> 00:05:20.947
Was it just you and your brother?

155
00:05:20.947 --> 00:05:21.579
<v VICTORIAN>We were ten.

156
00:05:21.579 --> 00:05:22.000
<v GELVIN>Ten?

157
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:22.217
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah.

158
00:05:22.217 --> 00:05:23.304
We’re ten in the family.

159
00:05:23.304 --> 00:05:23.522
Yeah.

160
00:05:23.522 --> 00:05:24.391
<v GELVIN>Ten in the family?

161
00:05:24.391 --> 00:05:24.609
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah.

162
00:05:24.609 --> 00:05:27.000
<v GELVIN>And were you the oldest, youngest, somewhere in the middle?

163
00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.113
<v VICTORIAN>I was about the fourth one.

164
00:05:29.113 --> 00:05:30.169
<v GELVIN>The fourth one.

165
00:05:30.169 --> 00:05:33.690
<v VICTORIAN>I had two sisters and two brothers older than me.

166
00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:34.042
<v GELVIN>Okay.

167
00:05:34.042 --> 00:05:34.394
Cool.

168
00:05:34.394 --> 00:05:34.746
Cool.

169
00:05:34.746 --> 00:05:40.028
And so you said after you came to Mossville you did some work in Texas.

170
00:05:40.028 --> 00:05:41.789
That’s where you learned pipefitting.

171
00:05:41.789 --> 00:05:42.141
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah.

172
00:05:42.141 --> 00:05:44.606
<v GELVIN>How long were you in Texas for?

173
00:05:44.606 --> 00:05:47.423
<v VICTORIAN>About a year and a half, two years.

174
00:05:47.423 --> 00:05:48.831
I came back home.

175
00:05:48.831 --> 00:05:51.648
We was carpooling back and forth to work.

176
00:05:51.648 --> 00:05:53.408
Got married, came back home.

177
00:05:53.408 --> 00:05:54.465
Got married young.

178
00:05:54.465 --> 00:05:59.042
Carpooling back and forth to Texas and this place started booming with jobs.

179
00:05:59.042 --> 00:06:00.803
Took a test with it.

180
00:06:00.803 --> 00:06:03.972
It’s Sasol now, but it used to be VCM.

181
00:06:03.972 --> 00:06:06.085
I took a test; passed it.

182
00:06:06.085 --> 00:06:08.901
And took a test at Olin; passed it.

183
00:06:08.901 --> 00:06:11.366
And they hired me at Olin first.

184
00:06:11.366 --> 00:06:17.000
They called me first and hired me at Olin and I worked there thirty-one years.

185
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:17.316
<v GELVIN>Wow.

186
00:06:17.316 --> 00:06:17.632
<v VICTORIAN>Right.

187
00:06:17.632 --> 00:06:18.895
A maintenance pipe fitter.

188
00:06:18.895 --> 00:06:19.526
Maintenance man.

189
00:06:19.526 --> 00:06:21.421
<v GELVIN>You’re still working there right now?

190
00:06:21.421 --> 00:06:23.000
<v VICTORIAN>Oh, no. I retired.

191
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:24.154
<v GELVIN>No, you retired.

192
00:06:24.154 --> 00:06:24.538
<v VICTORIAN>[Agrees].

193
00:06:24.538 --> 00:06:26.462
<v GELVIN>What year did you retire?

194
00:06:26.462 --> 00:06:26.846
<v VICTORIAN>2007.

195
00:06:26.846 --> 00:06:27.231
<v GELVIN>2007.

196
00:06:27.231 --> 00:06:28.000
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah.

197
00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:28.778
<v GELVIN>Pretty recently.

198
00:06:28.778 --> 00:06:29.167
<v VICTORIAN>[Agrees].

199
00:06:29.167 --> 00:06:29.556
<v GELVIN>Cool.

200
00:06:29.556 --> 00:06:31.889
<v VICTORIAN>They . . . Well they were shutting down.

201
00:06:31.889 --> 00:06:33.833
They gave me early retirement.

202
00:06:33.833 --> 00:06:35.000
<v GELVIN>Early retirement?

203
00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:37.872
<v VICTORIAN>They were shutting down the . . . Some of the units.

204
00:06:37.872 --> 00:06:39.787
So I took an early retirement.

205
00:06:39.787 --> 00:06:42.660
<v GELVIN>Did you go to church anywhere here in Mossville?

206
00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:45.532
Did your family belong to any of the churches?

207
00:06:45.532 --> 00:06:49.043
<v VICTORIAN>No, I went to the church at St. Bosco in Westlake.

208
00:06:49.043 --> 00:06:50.000
<v GELVIN>Oh, Westlake?

209
00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:54.075
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah that’s where I been going for the . . . Since I been here.

210
00:06:54.075 --> 00:06:54.755
<v GELVIN>Very cool.

211
00:06:54.755 --> 00:06:55.094
<v VICTORIAN>[Agrees].

212
00:06:55.094 --> 00:06:55.774
<v GELVIN>Very nice.

213
00:06:55.774 --> 00:06:59.509
So you briefly mentioned the canteen and the barbershop in Mossville.

214
00:06:59.509 --> 00:06:59.849
<v VICTORIAN>Yes.

215
00:06:59.849 --> 00:07:04.943
<v GELVIN>Do you have any particular memories from either of those that you’d like to share?

216
00:07:04.943 --> 00:07:05.283
<v VICTORIAN>Well . . .

217
00:07:05.283 --> 00:07:08.000
<v GELVIN>Any memories of going to the canteen?

218
00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:12.030
<v VICTORIAN>Well one of my cousins said we were going to check out this canteen.

219
00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:15.485
That’s right . . . Like more like teenagers right down the road over here.

220
00:07:15.485 --> 00:07:16.924
So we checked it out.

221
00:07:16.924 --> 00:07:20.379
Back then we had the old ’56, ’55 Fords and all that.

222
00:07:20.379 --> 00:07:21.530
I bought me one.

223
00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:22.970
And it was cool then.

224
00:07:22.970 --> 00:07:23.258
<v GELVIN>Yeah.

225
00:07:23.258 --> 00:07:27.000
<v VICTORIAN>Well mostly where the teenagers hung out, the canteen they called it.

226
00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:29.909
<v GELVIN>You were a little older when you went to go visit there?

227
00:07:29.909 --> 00:07:31.848
Were you a little older than the teenagers?

228
00:07:31.848 --> 00:07:33.303
Or were you like seventeen, eighteen?

229
00:07:33.303 --> 00:07:35.727
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah I was like seventeen when I first come here.

230
00:07:35.727 --> 00:07:35.970
<v GELVIN>Definitely.

231
00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:39.606
<v VICTORIAN>But I had a lot . . . A lot of family . . . Cousins like to hang out together.

232
00:07:39.606 --> 00:07:39.848
<v GELVIN>Yeah.

233
00:07:39.848 --> 00:07:42.030
So would y’all go there to listen to music?

234
00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:42.515
Or dance?

235
00:07:42.515 --> 00:07:43.000
Or . . .

236
00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:43.307
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah.

237
00:07:43.307 --> 00:07:44.228
Listen to music.

238
00:07:44.228 --> 00:07:47.604
They had a . . . They also had a cinema right up here.

239
00:07:47.604 --> 00:07:49.446
You’d take your dates or whatever.

240
00:07:49.446 --> 00:07:50.059
Right on . . .

241
00:07:50.059 --> 00:07:50.673
<v GELVIN>Right at . . .

242
00:07:50.673 --> 00:07:51.901
<v VICTORIAN>On the [Highway] 90.

243
00:07:51.901 --> 00:07:52.515
<v GELVIN>On 90?

244
00:07:52.515 --> 00:07:54.050
<v VICTORIAN>Right on 90 right here.

245
00:07:54.050 --> 00:07:59.267
They had a big cinema just as you go down Prater [Road] and a right . . . A left.

246
00:07:59.267 --> 00:08:01.723
About . . . Not far they had a cinema there.

247
00:08:01.723 --> 00:08:05.099
<v GELVIN>Do you have any particular memories of going to the cinema?

248
00:08:05.099 --> 00:08:10.931
Like do you have . . . I don’t know I guess maybe all the movies might be a blur by now.

249
00:08:10.931 --> 00:08:14.000
<v VICTORIAN>The popcorn, the smooching, you know how that goes.

250
00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:14.741
<v GELVIN>Ooh! [Laughs].

251
00:08:14.741 --> 00:08:15.111
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah.

252
00:08:15.111 --> 00:08:18.444
Sometime we’d go . . . Two or three couples . . . Two couples.

253
00:08:18.444 --> 00:08:19.185
Double date.

254
00:08:19.185 --> 00:08:20.296
Stuff like that.

255
00:08:20.296 --> 00:08:20.667
<v GELVIN>Cool.

256
00:08:20.667 --> 00:08:21.037
Cool.

257
00:08:21.037 --> 00:08:21.407
<v VICTORIAN>[Agrees].

258
00:08:21.407 --> 00:08:21.778
<v GELVIN>Awesome.

259
00:08:21.778 --> 00:08:27.704
Well in your time that you spent in Mossville, and then Texas for a little while,

260
00:08:27.704 --> 00:08:34.000
