261
00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:34.867
<v VICTORIAN>Oh, yes.

262
00:08:34.867 --> 00:08:36.600
A lot of changes.

263
00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:37.033
<v GELVIN>Yeah?

264
00:08:37.033 --> 00:08:40.500
Anything in particular you’d want to talk about?

265
00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:40.933
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah.

266
00:08:40.933 --> 00:08:45.700
Well it’s . . . Before that happened, I went to work with BFI.

267
00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:47.000
<v GELVIN>What’s BFI?

268
00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:49.434
<v VICTORIAN>BFI is a waste management.

269
00:08:49.434 --> 00:08:49.921
<v GELVIN>Okay.

270
00:08:49.921 --> 00:08:55.763
<v VICTORIAN>And they was digging holes, dumping stuff, and just filling stuff up.

271
00:08:55.763 --> 00:09:00.145
And I know some of that stuff was contaminated.

272
00:09:00.145 --> 00:09:00.632
<v GELVIN>Yeah.

273
00:09:00.632 --> 00:09:10.855
<v VICTORIAN>Because then I quit, but I know where a lot of the holes at now, but it’s fenced in and everything.

274
00:09:10.855 --> 00:09:24.000
But I know a lot of that was going on and so when I heard people talking about how the water was bad, I knew why.

275
00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:24.417
<v GELVIN>Yeah.

276
00:09:24.417 --> 00:09:26.917
You’d already seen it I guess.

277
00:09:26.917 --> 00:09:27.333
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah.

278
00:09:27.333 --> 00:09:27.750
Right.

279
00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:31.083
I already seen it going in the ground.

280
00:09:31.083 --> 00:09:37.750
So even the barrels with crosses on it . . . The orange barrels with the crosses on it.

281
00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:38.167
Yes.

282
00:09:38.167 --> 00:09:40.667
So I always drank bottled water.

283
00:09:40.667 --> 00:09:46.083
I can pinpoint where them holes at, but they all fenced in now.

284
00:09:46.083 --> 00:09:51.917
They . . . Somebody else got them, but I work for BFI like five, six years.

285
00:09:51.917 --> 00:09:54.000
<v GELVIN>Right here in Mossville?

286
00:09:54.000 --> 00:10:00.517
<v VICTORIAN>No, they had a business place right up here and they had a pit right up here.

287
00:10:00.517 --> 00:10:02.433
Had two of them possibly.

288
00:10:02.433 --> 00:10:07.033
They had another one back here, but they had one right here.

289
00:10:07.033 --> 00:10:09.717
They had one in Cameron, well Hackberry.

290
00:10:09.717 --> 00:10:12.783
They caught up with them over there though.

291
00:10:12.783 --> 00:10:13.167
<v GELVIN>Hackberry?

292
00:10:13.167 --> 00:10:22.367
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah they never did catch up with them for dumping that hazardous stuff here, but they caught up with them over there in Hackberry.

293
00:10:22.367 --> 00:10:22.750
<v GELVIN>Yeah.

294
00:10:22.750 --> 00:10:23.133
<v VICTORIAN>[Agrees].

295
00:10:23.133 --> 00:10:25.050
So I know about that.

296
00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:25.433
<v GELVIN>Yeah.

297
00:10:25.433 --> 00:10:28.883
<v VICTORIAN>So . . . Ashamed to say, but that’s how it is.

298
00:10:28.883 --> 00:10:31.950
That’s how they picked on us right here.

299
00:10:31.950 --> 00:10:35.783
And here we are all the plants coming in still.

300
00:10:35.783 --> 00:10:40.000
It’s not going to be a Mossville no more, so.

301
00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:42.323
<v GELVIN>So does your whole family still live in Mossville?

302
00:10:42.323 --> 00:10:42.581
<v VICTORIAN>Partially . . .

303
00:10:42.581 --> 00:10:43.871
<v GELVIN>Do any of your siblings . . .?

304
00:10:43.871 --> 00:10:45.419
<v VICTORIAN>Partially some of them moved out.

305
00:10:45.419 --> 00:10:46.968
I’m fixing to move out myself.

306
00:10:46.968 --> 00:10:48.000
<v GELVIN>Oh, are you?

307
00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:48.320
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah.

308
00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:50.240
<v GELVIN>Where are you planning on heading?

309
00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:52.480
<v VICTORIAN>I’m going to head . . . It’s almost DeQuincy.

310
00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:53.120
<v GELVIN>In DeQuincy.

311
00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:54.080
<v VICTORIAN>High Hope Road.

312
00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:54.400
Yeah.

313
00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:56.320
I’m getting out of here, too.

314
00:10:56.320 --> 00:10:56.640
Yeah.

315
00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:56.960
<v GELVIN>Yeah.

316
00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:01.760
<v VICTORIAN>Hell if I do, and hell if I don’t, so I got to do something.

317
00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:03.360
<v GELVIN>Got to head out there.

318
00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:04.000
<v VICTORIAN>[Agrees].

319
00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:04.524
<v GELVIN>Got you.

320
00:11:04.524 --> 00:11:06.881
Well . . . Okay, so just backtracking just a little bit.

321
00:11:06.881 --> 00:11:08.452
So we talked about the canteen.

322
00:11:08.452 --> 00:11:09.500
You mentioned the barbershop.

323
00:11:09.500 --> 00:11:13.429
Were there any other particular places in Mossville that you have any special memories in?

324
00:11:13.429 --> 00:11:19.452
Or any buildings or just places in Mossville that were important to you or you used to spend a lot of time at?

325
00:11:19.452 --> 00:11:24.429
<v VICTORIAN>I spent a lot of time . . . outdoors hunting and fishing in the woods and stuff because I liked that.

326
00:11:24.429 --> 00:11:26.000
I grew up doing that.

327
00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:29.770
<v GELVIN>A lot of the woods right around here?

328
00:11:29.770 --> 00:11:30.241
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah.

329
00:11:30.241 --> 00:11:32.598
The woods right around here.

330
00:11:32.598 --> 00:11:38.253
And it was all open then there wasn’t no posted signs nowhere.

331
00:11:38.253 --> 00:11:40.138
So that’s the things.

332
00:11:40.138 --> 00:11:47.207
And when they start digging all the ponds and everything, this is good fishing stuff.

333
00:11:47.207 --> 00:11:47.678
<v GELVIN>Yeah.

334
00:11:47.678 --> 00:11:48.149
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah.

335
00:11:48.149 --> 00:11:52.862
Some of them they covered up with stuff though, but . . .

336
00:11:52.862 --> 00:11:53.805
<v GELVIN>Got you.

337
00:11:53.805 --> 00:12:01.816
Well when your family first moved here, did y’all keep a garden or any livestock or animals?

338
00:12:01.816 --> 00:12:02.759
<v VICTORIAN>Oh, yeah.

339
00:12:02.759 --> 00:12:04.644
We kept a garden.

340
00:12:04.644 --> 00:12:06.057
Chickens, ducks, guineas . . .

341
00:12:06.057 --> 00:12:07.000
<v GELVIN>Guineas?

342
00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:09.162
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah because we come from the country.

343
00:12:09.162 --> 00:12:12.868
That’s how we grew up with live off the land a lot.

344
00:12:12.868 --> 00:12:13.485
<v GELVIN>Got you.

345
00:12:13.485 --> 00:12:13.794
<v VICTORIAN>[Agrees].

346
00:12:13.794 --> 00:12:14.412
<v GELVIN>Got you.

347
00:12:14.412 --> 00:12:15.029
Very cool.

348
00:12:15.029 --> 00:12:21.824
Well yeah, before we wrap this up, just want to ask you if there’s anything else you’d like to say about Mossville?

349
00:12:21.824 --> 00:12:28.000
Like anything in particular that’s special to you about this place, or anything else you’d really like to share?

350
00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:30.269
<v VICTORIAN>Well everybody’s like family in Mossville.

351
00:12:30.269 --> 00:12:40.479
I mean everybody’s just like families . . . are family and I hate to see us breaking up this way and everybody scattering out everywhere going to different directions.

352
00:12:40.479 --> 00:12:49.933
I was hoping we could all . . . If they could have worked something out where they could put us all back in another Mossville somewhere else.

353
00:12:49.933 --> 00:12:52.958
That’s what I . . . I’d made a suggestion, but . . .

354
00:12:52.958 --> 00:12:55.227
<v GELVIN>So y’all could all stay together?

355
00:12:55.227 --> 00:12:55.605
<v VICTORIAN>Yeah.

356
00:12:55.605 --> 00:12:57.496
Right, but that’s not happening.

357
00:12:57.496 --> 00:12:58.252
<v GELVIN>Got you.

358
00:12:58.252 --> 00:12:59.008
Got you.

359
00:12:59.008 --> 00:13:03.168
<v VICTORIAN>My two kids . . . I have two kids that grew up here.

